Day 36 (2026-11-05) - the Linux port begins: Lazarus 4.8, the file manager layer (VersariteViewer 1.4)

The projects moved to a new computer (user), Lazarus 4.8 there; everything compiles. Goal (user):
a Linux version (Ubuntu 24.04, GNOME on Wayland) that works with Double Commander, which needs a
small patch so programs can ask it what it shows. Decided: one code base (no second repository),
the port on a branch "linux" of the existing repository, merged when it runs on the user's Ubuntu;
the Double Commander patch in a fork of doublecmd/doublecmd.

1. docs\Linux_port_inventory.md: the Windows-only parts unit by unit and their Linux replacements.
   Widget set GTK2 (an X11 program under XWayland: it may place its own window, OpenGL through
   GLX); what Wayland takes away (global hotkey, other programs' windows).
2. Lazarus 4.8 built here from the official GitHub mirror (tag lazarus_4_8, FPC 3.2.2), so the
   test builds match the user's; its OpenGL example runs on GTK2 under a virtual X display
   (Mesa llvmpipe). Ubuntu needs libgtk2.0-dev, libgl-dev, libglu1-mesa.
3. The file manager layer (step 2 of the port): uFileManager.TFileManager - is it running /
   shown, its program, open a folder, place / move its window, the folder and file under its
   cursor (Poll), the two panels' items (Combine GIFs), Info, Restart. uTotalCommander.
   TTotalCommanderLink is the Windows implementation (the old functions and uTCFollow behind it),
   registered in its initialization section; FileManager is nil where none is registered.
   TMView (following, the TC badge, subfolders while following, Combine GIFs, the D line) and
   TMainForm (side by side from the settings screen and the menu, fullscreen with side by side,
   two screens) call only FileManager now; no Total Commander window handle is left in them.
   Behaviour unchanged; the notes still say "Total Commander" (they follow the file manager's
   name when Double Commander comes).
   Checked: both editions build (win64); TestNavigation 153, TestMouse 98, TestSort 172,
   TestStl 25, TestGifJoin 32, TestExtensions 80 - all pass. New TestFileManager.lpr (plain fpc,
   in build_test.bat): Total Commander registered on Windows and every question "no" while it
   isn't running; another implementation registered in its place is made once and answers -
   26 checks on Windows, 15 on Linux (it compiles and runs there natively).
4. Step 3 of the port (user: "go ahead"): VersariteViewer builds for Linux (Lazarus 4.8, GTK2) and
   runs. Most units already had non-Windows branches; the first Linux build compiled at once.
   Linux branches made today:
   - uGLRenderer: OpenGL functions through glXGetProcAddressARB; glGenerateMipmap only with
     OpenGL 3 or the framebuffer extension (GLX gives an address for any name). Before: no
     mipmaps, no filter shader on Linux.
   - uMemoryGuard, uImageCache: the computer's memory from sysinfo(2) (was a fixed guess).
   - uStopwatch: the monotonic clock (clock_gettime), the process age from /proc.
   - uWatchdog: the deadline ends the process with _exit (FpExit), no finalization.
   - uSingleInstance: a Unix domain socket in the abstract namespace, one per edition and
     user; a new start hands its path over and ends (77 ms), the running one answers "OK";
     no answer within 5 s: it runs on its own. Accepted on a 100 ms timer, no thread.
   - uClipboardImage: Copy image puts a PNG (image/png) on the clipboard.
   - uMView: free disk space by statfs(2) of each folder (network file systems not asked);
     IsFullPath knows "/".
   - uMainForm: fullscreen through the window manager (WindowState wsFullScreen, EWMH), which
     GNOME honours under Wayland as well.
   Checked under a virtual X display with a window manager (Openbox), Mesa llvmpipe:
   fullscreen; JPEG, PNG, TIFF, animated GIF (11 frames), STL drawn by OpenGL with mipmaps and
   the filter shader; stepping through a folder tree with the wheel; Esc to the settings screen;
   on "Mouse & keys" / "Try it here" the side buttons (X1 zoom mode, X2 rotate mode) and the
   tilt wheel (previous / next folder) arrive under GTK2; Ctrl+C gives an 800 x 526 PNG on the
   clipboard; a second start hands its file to the running one.
   Tests natively on Linux: TestNavigation 153, TestMouse 98, TestSort 172, TestFileManager 15,
   TestStl 25, TestGifJoin 32, TestExtensions 80 - all pass. Windows: both editions build, the
   same tests pass (TestFileManager 26), the program starts under Wine. The Image Lab builds for
   Linux too (not tried further).
   Noted for later: paths are compared without case (SameText), Linux names are case-sensitive;
   Documents / Pictures are ~/Documents and ~/Pictures (localised XDG folders not read yet);
   the GPU renderer also runs on Mesa's software OpenGL (llvmpipe), usable for these sizes.
5. Double Commander (user: "test internally without publishing, then ask opinions"). Its
   current source (master 0d3888b, 2026-11-04) built here for GTK2 with Lazarus 4.8. A proposed
   remote control channel added to it (C:\projects\double commander: the patch, the changed
   files, README): uRemoteControl, off by default (Behaviours/RemoteControl); a Unix socket in
   $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (else /tmp/doublecmd-<uid>, 0700), the caller's user id checked; pairing
   (the first HELLO asks Double Commander's user, a token is handed out); GET ACTIVE / PATH /
   CURSOR / VIEW / WINDOW, SET WINDOW, CMD from an allow-list (no deleting, moving, running);
   cm_MakeDir path= without the dialog; the thumbnail commands with state=on / off.
   VersariteViewer: uDoubleCommander.TDoubleCommanderLink, the file manager on Linux (registered
   there; Total Commander stays on Windows); the pairing answer is waited for without blocking,
   every question at most 300 ms; the token in VersariteViewer.dctoken next to the ini. Notes,
   menu entries, the settings screen's button and the badge ("DC") name the file manager in use
   (uFileManager.FMName). uSingleInstance: only the same user's programs are listened to
   (SO_PEERCRED).
   Checked together under a virtual display: Double Commander asked, Yes, token kept; VV follows
   its cursor (TIFF, animated GIF); "Side by side with Double Commander" puts VV on the left
   half and Double Commander on the right. Both editions still build for Windows and Linux.
   User: Double Commander runs on Windows too - with this two-way channel it may replace Total
   Commander there as well (needs the named pipe on Double Commander's side).
